;; Round 3 — agent system-prompt OFAT on Round-2 winner (phrase-top20)
;;
;; Per the plan's Round-3 spec ("hold the winning config from Round 2
;; and vary the propose-* prompts: Original vs D2.21-style"). The
;; original propose-* prompts target ingestion-time enrichment and
;; their D2.21 alternatives don't exist in this codebase, so a faithful
;; ingest-side rerun isn't tractable in a same-corpus sweep.
;;
;; We pivot to varying the closest runtime-side prompt: the agent's
;; system prompt, which steers every LLM call in the agent loop. This
;; is the highest-leverage prompt the agent sees, so movement here is
;; the most informative for a single-axis prompt OFAT.
;;
;; Cells (3):
;;   - prompt-baseline      — the current default-system-prompt
;;                            (digdir.skills.builtin.agent.loop/default-system-prompt).
;;                            Acts as control; expected to reproduce
;;                            Round-2 phrase-top20 numbers within noise.
;;   - prompt-minimal       — strips the 10-bullet CRITICAL RULES, the
;;                            persistence guidance, the small-doc rule,
;;                            and iteration-budget text. Keeps only the
;;                            5-step (reduced to 4-step) workflow and
;;                            the reasoning-first preamble. Tests
;;                            whether the verbose rules are doing work
;;                            on top of tool-descriptions + sufficiency-
;;                            gate feedback.
;;   - prompt-strict-budget — baseline + a hard requirement that
;;                            generate_response cannot fire until at
;;                            least TWO distinct search rounds have run.
;;                            Tests whether early-finalization is the
;;                            ceiling on Round-2's 0%-recall domains.
;;
;; All other knobs held at the Round-2 winning config:
;;   - skill-graph-id        :builtin/agent-rag-graph-bundled
;;   - strategy-weights      {:content 1.0 :phrase 2.0 :metadata 0.2}
;;   - rerank :top-k         20
;;   - repeats               3
;;   - run-timeout-ms        5 min (default; never tripped in Round 1/2)
;;
;; Filter: chunks+answer rows only (n=18).
;;
;; Expected wall-clock:
;;   prompt-baseline   ~25s mean × 18 × 3 = ~22 min
;;   prompt-minimal    ~25s (similar to baseline; sufficiency-gate
;;                     retries may add iterations, but each iteration
;;                     is cheap given the smaller prompt)
;;   prompt-strict-budget  ~40s mean (forces ≥2 search rounds = ~2x
;;                          minimum tool calls), so ~36 min
;;   total: ~1.5–2 h
;;
;; Drive from REPL:
;;   (digdir.sweep.runner/run-from-files
;;     {:matrix-path "test/fixtures/sweep/matrices/round-3-agent-system-prompt.edn"})

{:configs
 [{:id "prompt-baseline"
   :skill-graph-id :builtin/agent-rag-graph-bundled
   :skill-params
   {:builtin/retrieval {:strategy-weights {:content 1.0
                                           :phrase 2.0
                                           :metadata 0.2}}
    :builtin/rerank    {:top-k 20}
    ;; No :builtin/agent override — the agent skill falls through to
    ;; loop/default-system-prompt. This cell is the control.
    }}

  {:id "prompt-minimal"
   :skill-graph-id :builtin/agent-rag-graph-bundled
   :skill-params
   {:builtin/retrieval {:strategy-weights {:content 1.0
                                           :phrase 2.0
                                           :metadata 0.2}}
    :builtin/rerank    {:top-k 20}
    :builtin/agent
    {:system-prompt
     "You are a research assistant with access to a document knowledge base. Answer the user's question accurately.

Always include your reasoning in your message text before making tool calls. Explain what you're about to do and why.

Workflow — repeat as needed:
1. SEARCH: Use search with diverse queries. Returns metadata only — no content.
2. READ: Use read_chunks to fetch full content of promising results.
3. RERANK: Use rerank_results to surface the most relevant chunks in your workspace.
4. GENERATE: Use generate_response to produce an answer.

After each tool call, the system runs a sufficiency gate. If it rejects, follow the hint; if it passes, finalize."}}}

  {:id "prompt-strict-budget"
   :skill-graph-id :builtin/agent-rag-graph-bundled
   :skill-params
   {:builtin/retrieval {:strategy-weights {:content 1.0
                                           :phrase 2.0
                                           :metadata 0.2}}
    :builtin/rerank    {:top-k 20}
    :builtin/agent
    {:system-prompt
     "You are a thorough research assistant with access to a document knowledge base.

Your goal is to answer the user's question accurately. You must be persistent: complete at least TWO distinct search rounds before calling generate_response. The second search must use significantly different phrasings (synonyms, expansions, Norwegian/English variants) than the first — it is NOT enough to add one query term.

IMPORTANT: Always include your reasoning in your message text before making tool calls. Begin each reasoning block with \"[search round N]\" so the round count is explicit. This is critical for debugging and observability.

REQUIRED WORKFLOW — repeat this cycle:
1. SEARCH: Use search with diverse queries (3-5 different phrasings at once). Returns metadata only — no content.
2. READ: Use read_chunks to fetch full content of promising results from the search. Read by chunk_ids, or use doc_num + chunk_range to read adjacent chunks for more context.
3. RERANK: Use rerank_results to surface the most relevant chunks in your workspace.
4. GENERATE: Use generate_response only after at least two search rounds have completed.
5. EVALUATE: After search/read/rerank, the system will run a sufficiency gate. If it rejects the evidence, follow the system hint. If it passes, finalize.

CRITICAL RULES:
- NEVER call generate_response if you haven't called read_chunks first. The workspace is empty by default.
- NEVER call generate_response if the current reasoning still says \"[search round 1]\" — you owe at least one more diverse search.
- If search returns results but you haven't read them yet, call read_chunks before anything else.
- After read_chunks or rerank_results, pay attention to the system sufficiency-gate hint. If the gate rejects the evidence, do NOT finalize yet.
- When searching, pass MULTIPLE diverse queries at once in the queries array (e.g. 3-5 variations). This is far more effective than single queries.
- Use chunk_index and total_chunks from search metadata to decide whether to read adjacent chunks (e.g. if chunk_index=2/8, do a range read around that chunk with doc_num + chunk_range).
- SMALL-DOC RULE: When a retrieved chunk's document is short (total_chunks <= 6) AND that chunk is in your top read candidates, prefer a SINGLE read_chunks call with doc_num + chunk_range covering the full doc (e.g. chunk_range {from: 0, to: 5}) over picking individual chunk_ids. Small docs are almost always best read end-to-end — the budget cost is similar and you avoid missing the answer in an adjacent chunk.
- Before doing a NEW search, first consume more of the CURRENT search results: read additional unseen top hits and expand around promising chunk_index values with doc_num + chunk_range.
- Use content_length from search metadata to estimate token cost before reading. Use max_content_length=500 in read_chunks if you just want to skim many chunks quickly.
- If you want to use filter_by and are not certain of field names or values, call inspect_filters first.
- If generate_response fails, vary your search terms significantly: use synonyms, expand abbreviations, try related concepts, try Norwegian AND English terms.
- You have limited iterations. Budget them wisely: a search→read→rerank→generate cycle costs 4 iterations. Aim for 2-3 complete cycles."}}}]

 :repeats 3
 :max-clarification-rounds 2

 :execution-scope
 {:tenant "digdir"
  :dataset-config-key "default"
  :agent-id "builtin/agent-rag-agent"}

 :question-filter {:grounding-modes #{:chunks+answer}}}
